Answer: Synchronization is a locking mechanism implemented with the synchronized keyword. It ensures that, at any point in time, only one thread can access the protected shared resource operation on the same object. 35. Coding Question 1: Write a Java program in which three threads share one Bathroom object. Use synchronization so that only one thread can use the bathroom at a time.

```java
class Bathroom {
public synchronized void use() {
String name = Thread.currentThread().getName();
System.out.println(name + " has entered the bathroom");
try {
Thread.sleep(5000);
System.out.println(name + " is using the bathroom");
Thread.sleep(5000);
```

} catch (InterruptedException e) {

```java
e.printStackTrace();
}
System.out.println(name + " has exited the bathroom");
}
}
class Boy extends Thread {
private Bathroom bathroom;
```

Boy(Bathroom bathroom) {

```java
this.bathroom = bathroom;
}
@Override
public void run() {
bathroom.use();
}
}
class Girl extends Thread {
private Bathroom bathroom;
```

Girl(Bathroom bathroom) {

```java
this.bathroom = bathroom;
}
@Override
public void run() {
bathroom.use();
}
}
class Other extends Thread {
private Bathroom bathroom;
```

Other(Bathroom bathroom) {

```java
this.bathroom = bathroom;
}
@Override
public void run() {
bathroom.use();
}
}
public class ThreadSynchronizationDemo {
public static void main(String[] args) {
Bathroom bathroom = new Bathroom();
Boy boy = new Boy(bathroom);
Girl girl = new Girl(bathroom);
Other other = new Other(bathroom);
boy.setName("Boy");
girl.setName("Girl");
other.setName("Other");
boy.start();
girl.start();
other.start();
}
}
```

